# [FINDINGS] B0 — THE ACCEPTING BUNDLE (docs/design/findings/design.md §3.1,
# §2.7's second exhibit, abbreviated). Every B0 row appears once in a legal
# spelling: the `analysis` opener, a bundle-scope `include <other>` and prose
# `description`, a `freq` block with `encoding`, `serves` (two compile
# encodings on one line), ascending `row`s and a `provenance` whose
# `source exemplar` owes `bytes`/`sha256` but no `url` (design §0.10), a
# second bundle with an AUTHORED provenance owing neither, and a config
# naming ONE analysis. Nesting is FOUR frames deep here (file -> bundle ->
# freq -> provenance), which is the structure layer's third level (§3.1).
#
# LEG A ONLY: `analysis` is a HEAD declaration and the head has one parser
# (the seam ruling, w1_impl §1.1). tests/rxtsource/run_rxtsource_tests.sh's
# "[FINDINGS] B0" section asserts the rows, not just the exit code.
analysis weblog
    include <log>
    description |
        Our gateway logs, extending the shipped log bundle.
    freq
        question How often does each byte occur in our gateway logs?
        reader byte-rate
        analyzer pcrec-analyze 0.1 --scan freq
        encoding utf8
        serves byte-rate when byte,utf8 via unigram
        row 0a 1822
        row 20 124561
        row 61 54163
        provenance
            source exemplar
            retrieved 2026-09-26
            bytes 104857600
            sha256 3f00
analysis plain
    freq
        question How often does each byte occur?
        reader byte-rate
        analyzer authored
        encoding ascii
        serves byte-rate when byte via unigram
        row 00 2
        row ff 2
        provenance
            source authored
            retrieved 2026-09-26

config prod
    analysis weblog

pattern a
name a
m "a" 0 1
